WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.010
My name is Eldrick Jacobs.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.940
I've been making and repairing hats

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.273
for about three-plus years.

00:00:15.273 --> 00:00:16.860
Today I'll be repairing a hat

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.590
that is approximately 70-plus years old

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.258
while maintaining its character.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.250
First, I'm going to deconstruct the hat.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.403
We use a seam ripper,
and then we use snippers.

00:00:30.625 --> 00:00:32.788
The felt is in really great condition,

00:00:32.788 --> 00:00:35.070
but we have to be very
careful to make sure

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.080
that we don't poke any holes into the felt

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.753
or rip something that needn't be ripped.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.680
This hat belonged to my
client's grandfather.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.920
And when he passed, he
passed it down to his father.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.780
We're going to maintain the character

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.893
and patina of the hat and the hole.

00:00:51.893 --> 00:00:53.800
The client would like to keep the hole

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.105
to maintain the continuity
from generation to generation.

00:00:57.105 --> 00:00:58.254
The hat has a story,

00:00:58.254 --> 00:01:00.641
and he wants to tell that
story as he wears it.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.090
Next, I'm going to clean the felt.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.190
I use a little bit of alcohol
and a little bit of water.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:11.640
And then we use a hat brush
to brush away any debris

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.990
that may be present on
the surface of the felt.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:17.010
So, the alcohol does two things.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:18.660
It dissolves a little bit

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.450
of the bioresin that's inside of the felt,

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.740
making it a little bit more pliable

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.300
and more easy to work with,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.752
and then it gets rid of any bacteria

00:01:26.752 --> 00:01:29.197
that may be present on
the surface of the felt

00:01:29.197 --> 00:01:32.730
and, likewise, any mold
that may be present.

00:01:32.730 --> 00:01:34.290
And then we use the sponge

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:36.749
to get a little bit deeper into the felt.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:46.110
For the hat band, we spray
it with alcohol again,

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.150
and this allows me to go over it

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:50.834
with another sponge to really sort of

00:01:50.834 --> 00:01:54.124
clean up the hat band
and the loose threads.

00:01:54.124 --> 00:01:57.720
We really work to keep it
in its original condition.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:01:59.580
We want it, like the felt,

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:02.130
to maintain the character

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.715
that it has gained over
those years of wear.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:08.910
After we do that, we take snippers

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.997
and we snip any loose threads.

00:02:11.997 --> 00:02:14.670
Then we turn our attention
to reblocking the felt.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:18.185
Reblocking is the most crucial
part of remaking a hat.

00:02:18.185 --> 00:02:21.720
We take the felt and we
heat it up with some steam,

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.878
and then we stretch it over a block.

00:02:25.170 --> 00:02:27.665
We are choosing a block that'll give us

00:02:27.665 --> 00:02:29.752
the circumference of the client's head.

00:02:31.119 --> 00:02:33.235
What we're doing here is creating

00:02:33.235 --> 00:02:36.101
the basic shape that we
want to give the hat,

00:02:36.101 --> 00:02:37.756
called an open crown.

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.404
And then once we have it
stretched over the block,

00:02:43.404 --> 00:02:46.590
we'll grab a hatter's
knot and a pusher downer,

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.140
which is the tool that we
use to take the hatter's knot

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:51.436
to the bottom of the hat block.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:56.610
Here is where we are creating
the 90-degree break angle

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:59.076
between the crown and the felt.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:04.440
The hat would be irreparable

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:07.680
if the felt itself was
starting to disintegrate,

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.921
particularly around the break line.

00:03:09.921 --> 00:03:13.500
That would create an issue
for reblocking the hat.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:18.030
I'm using a brim cutter,
and it allows for us

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:22.800
to decide how wide or how
short we want the brim.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.760
This brim has to be regulated
down a half an inch.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.385
The general shape will stay the same,

00:03:29.385 --> 00:03:30.485
but we are making sure

00:03:30.485 --> 00:03:33.939
that the hat frames my
client's face nicely.

00:03:38.967 --> 00:03:41.850
And then I'll use a fine-grit sandpaper

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.413
to really refine the edge.

00:03:45.752 --> 00:03:48.292
In the brim-cutting process,

00:03:48.292 --> 00:03:51.421
you don't always get the cleanest edge,

00:03:51.421 --> 00:03:53.130
so the sandpaper allows for us

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.013
to really create sharp edges on the hat.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:02.130
And finally, we will create a sweatband.

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:04.435
We cannot reuse the old sweatband.

00:04:04.435 --> 00:04:06.120
All we have left is the reed.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.490
And so we have to replace the sweatband

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.620
with a new piece of leather.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:12.269
The sweatband is critically important

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:14.460
to maintaining the integrity of the hat.

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:16.350
It really holds the hat together.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:18.973
But more importantly,
it allows for a barrier

00:04:18.973 --> 00:04:22.622
between the wearer's
forehead and the felt itself.

00:04:22.622 --> 00:04:24.637
Hatters can do a lot of things,

00:04:24.637 --> 00:04:26.212
but getting sweat out of felt

00:04:26.212 --> 00:04:28.020
is quite a difficult feat.

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.223
And then, finally, we
shape the crown of the hat.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:37.890
We use steam again.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:41.250
Steam really allows for
the felt fibers to open up.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:43.980
There are two material
components that make up the felt:

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.020
there's the individual fur fibers,

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.015
and then there is the bioresin.

00:04:48.015 --> 00:04:50.790
Felt without the bioresin
would be very flimsy

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.920
and not have very much structure to it.

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.590
We are really opening that
up and softening it a bit

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:58.523
so that we can get the desired shape.

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.660
I'm excited about this
particular restoration,

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:09.540
and I think that my client
will be over the moon

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:11.550
and overjoyed that he has a hat

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:15.960
that looks and feels like
his grandfather's hat

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.173
and also has the patina and the character

00:05:19.173 --> 00:05:22.560
that comes with a 70-plus-year-old hat.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.135
The only reason this hat would need

00:05:24.135 --> 00:05:26.776
to be brought back into our shop

00:05:26.776 --> 00:05:29.316
is if we need to do hat maintenance,

00:05:29.316 --> 00:05:31.800
particularly with the sweatband.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.660
In general, this hat will last

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:37.573
as long as my client
wants to wear the hat.

